Dropped along with the bespoke infrastructure
=============================================

Two files were carried for a while as hand-written tests with a helper
module of their own, because what they check is not a workload with an
invariant.  Both have been removed, together with that module: a second
way to write a test is not worth maintaining for two files.  What that
costs, and what it would take to get it back:

  900_serializable.pl   The gapless-sequence and write-skew SSI tests.
                        This is the only thing that covered predicate
                        locks moving from an index to the heap during
                        DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY (see REGRESSIONS,
                        4c9d0901f13); nothing covers it now.  It wants
                        an isolation-style test rather than a stress
                        one -- the shape is deterministic, and the old
                        file spent most of its length re-implementing
                        what an isolation spec gives for free.

  901_pg_dump.pl        Dump and restore is an external process, not a
                        pgbench script, and its invariant is a
                        comparison between two databases.  This one is a
                        plausible check plugin: a 'final' check that
                        shells out to pg_dump and diffs, which is
                        exactly the shape the check registry already
                        supports.  It found a real problem before it was
                        removed -- REPACK CONCURRENTLY letting pg_dump
                        emit an empty table under writers -- so bringing
                        it back as a plugin is worth doing.

Gaps this rewrite has not closed
================================

These were covered by the old files and are not covered to the same
depth yet.  They are listed rather than quietly dropped, because the old
files are gone from the tree and only the git history has them:

  cross-database decoding     027 ran REPACK churn in one database while
                              a slot decoded another.  Needs a load
                              targeting a second database.

  REPACK's MVCC tolerance     Stress::MVCC now expresses the caveat in
                              one place, switched by whether the
                              scenario's rotation carries a command
                              declared mvcc_safe => 0, and the checks
                              that read a repacked relation use it.  What
                              is still open is the loads: one of them had
                              to be written to survive an empty read (see
                              subscriber_delete_reinsert), and others may
                              need the same.
